Q: Up to what order value can a purchase executive approve on their own, and who approves above that?
   found: ['POL-PUR-001 §5.2', 'POL-APR-002 §5.1', 'POL-APR-002 §1', 'POL-PUR-001 §5.4']
   answer: A purchase executive's limit is £250,000, as shown by Emma Clarke's limit in the example [POL-APR-002 §5.1]. An order above that limit goes to the next person up whose limit covers it, such as a purchase manager with a £1,000,000 limit [POL-PUR-001 §5.2; POL-APR-002 §5.1]. 

Q: Can we order battery packs from a supplier that has no UN 38.3 certificate on file?
   found: ['POL-PUR-001 §6.2', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.1', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.5', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.7']
   answer: No. Battery packs are lithium-cell parts, which "may be bought only from a supplier holding a valid UN 38.3 certificate on file in Ledgerline" [POL-PUR-001 §6.2]. Ledgerline will not block such an order, so the person raising it and the approver must check the certificate [POL-SUP-003 §4.7]. 

Q: What happens to an order that is charged to a closed cost centre?
   found: ['MAN-PUR-72 §8.1', 'MAN-PUR-72 §11.9', 'MAN-PUR-72 §8.2', 'POL-PUR-001 §4.2']
   answer: An order charged to a closed cost centre is saved as a Draft (DR) with warning W-CC-114, and nothing is sent to anyone [MAN-PUR-72 §11.9]. Ledgerline does not refuse the document, but a closed cost centre cannot carry new spend [MAN-PUR-72 §8.2]. A Draft order is never sent to the supplier [POL-PUR- 

Q: How recent must a supplier's UN 38.3 certificate be?
   found: ['AMD-SUP-2026-02 §1.1', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.1', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.6', 'POL-SUP-003 §4.2']
   answer: A supplier's UN 38.3 certificate must have been issued within the last 12 months, and its valid-to date must be at least 60 days after the order date, per the replacement §4.4 of POL-SUP-003 [AMD-SUP-2026-02 §1.1]. This rule takes effect on 1 September 2026 [AMD-SUP-2026-02 §1.1]. 


